# toke — outstanding work register

Everything left incomplete against a plan acceptance criterion, or
claimed-but-unverified, across Phases 0–9 and the work since.

Maintained alongside [IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md](IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md). The plan
says what the phases are; this file says what they did not deliver. Add an
entry the moment something is skipped — a deferral that lives only in a commit
message is a deferral nobody will find.

Sections 1–6 are work. **Section 7 is not**: it holds choices that were
considered and declined, so they stop reading as debt and nobody rediscovers
them as problems.

Status: `OPEN` · `BLOCKED` (needs another task first) · `CLOSED` (with what
closed it) · `DONE`

Last audited: 2026-09-22, against the code at v0.2.0. Entries closed by later work are
marked CLOSED with what closed them; deliberate choices live in §7 rather than here.

---

## 1. Defects — introduced, not deferred

### DEF-1 · Grouped objects disappear from the canvas · DONE · 2026-09-21
`groupSelection` moves children into the group node, so they leave the
top-level array. `StudioCanvas.buildFabricObject` returns `null` for
`kind: 'group'`, and the render loop only walks top-level nodes — so the
children are never drawn.

The P3 e2e asserted the layers **tree** showed three items after grouping and
never checked the canvas, which is exactly how this got through.

**Fixed:** `renderableNodes` flattens groups to leaves in paint order — groups
hold absolute coordinates, so no transform stack is needed. Selecting a group
now selects every leaf inside it.

The viewport exposes `data-fabric-objects`, so browser tests can assert what
Fabric actually holds rather than what the tree claims. Two regression tests
cover place/undo and group/ungroup.

### DEF-2 · Canvas text edits may not reach the store · CLOSED · not a defect
Investigated and disproved. `object:modified` does fire when Fabric's text
editor is exited, `commitFromFabric` reads `object.text`, and the edit reaches
the scene node — which is what the PDF renders.

Two things the investigation turned up that were worth writing down:

- Fabric enters editing on a click against an **already-selected** text
  object, not on a double-click. A text node is placed with its top-left at
  the click point, so clicking that same point again lands on the top-left
  resize handle: the object never sees a mousedown and never enters editing.
- Keystrokes typed while no editor is open reach the window shortcut handler,
  so "Rectangle" would switch tools four times. `isTextEntry` already guards
  this once Fabric's textarea exists.

Covered by `e2e/textedit.spec.ts`: entering the editor, the edit reaching the
node, an undo/redo round trip, and that typing does not fire tool shortcuts.

### DEF-3 · First edit shifted the canvas 40px · DONE · 2026-09-22
The dirty flag appearing in the header made it wrap to a second line at
1024–1440px, pushing the canvas down under the pointer mid-gesture — what
looked like pen misplacement. The design switcher moved to the left sidebar,
the header is `flex-nowrap`, and the flag reserves its width. Regression test
in `e2e/shell.spec.ts` at both widths.

### DEF-4 · A change of artboard size left the canvas ignoring the mouse · DONE · 2026-09-23
The Fabric canvas was rebuilt whenever the artboard size changed, but every
listener effect had attached to the old one and never re-ran. Opening the
tent-fold template (85 × 110mm) produced a canvas that could not be clicked,
dragged or zoomed. Found by VER-1. The canvas is now created once; the
artboard rectangle is resized in its own effect. `e2e/remount.spec.ts`.

### DEF-5 · Moving or resizing several objects threw them across the artboard · DONE · 2026-09-23
Members of a Fabric `ActiveSelection` hold coordinates relative to the
selection. `commitFromFabric` stored them as scene coordinates, and the
store → Fabric sync wrote scene coordinates back as relative ones. Found by
VER-3. `sceneTransform` and `placeInSelection` in `StudioCanvas.tsx` convert
through the selection's matrix in both directions. `e2e/selection.spec.ts`
covers move and resize.

### DEF-6 · A full disk was reported as a saved snapshot · DONE · 2026-09-23
IndexedDB writes settled on the request's success. Chrome checks quota at
commit, after that success, so a QuotaExceededError aborted the transaction
unnoticed and recovery silently held stale data. Writes now settle on the
transaction. Found by VER-6; `e2e/quota.spec.ts` produces the genuine error.

---

## 2. Blocked on a later phase

### BLK-1 · Image tool · CLOSED
The asset store is content-addressed, the PDF renderer embeds PNG/JPEG from
bytes handed to it on the export job, and the UI is built: a file input on the
toolbar (`ImageImport.tsx`), `FabricImage` construction from `objectUrlFor`,
retain wired to node creation, and asset bytes collected into `packProject`.

Verified end to end in `e2e/images.spec.ts`: place, resize, choose a fit mode,
export a PDF carrying one image XObject for the whole sheet, and reopen a
saved `.toke` with the image intact.

Two follow-ons remain open under their own entries — INC-34 (no crop
rectangle) and INC-35 (no aspect lock on resize).

### BLK-2 · Pen / path tool · CLOSED
Click for corner anchors, drag for smooth curves with mirrored handles, click
the first anchor to close, Enter to finish, Escape to abandon. The draft stays
out of the scene graph until it is finished, so a half-drawn path cannot reach
the PDF, and the whole path is a single undo.

Geometry lives in `engine/canvas/pen.ts`. Its bounds are the exact bezier
extrema rather than anchors plus handles: Fabric recentres a path on its tight
curve bounds while the PDF draws `d` from the node origin, so a box that
included unreached handle tips would place the path differently on screen and
on paper.

Editing the anchors of an existing path is not built — a finished path can be
moved, resized, recoloured and deleted, but its points are fixed. Recorded as
INC-38.

### BLK-3 · Token-bound indicator in the layers tree · CLOSED
`const bound = false` is gone; the tree reads `isTokenised(node.text)`, and a
group counts as bound when anything inside it is. The badge renders the word
"bound" as well as the `--bound` colour, per §4.7.

### BLK-4 · Diagnostics panel wiring · CLOSED by P9.2 · from P2.1, P3
Errors currently surface as one-off `role="alert"` paragraphs. Every
`AppError` already carries `objectId`; nothing consumes it yet.

- **Accept:** every error reaches the diagnostics panel, and clicking one with
  an `objectId` selects that canvas object.

