## 3. Incomplete against a plan acceptance criterion

These were signed off as phase-complete with parts missing. Each names the
criterion it fails.

### INC-1 · Rulers · CLOSED
> "Rulers in the active display unit with a live cursor indicator. Ruler ticks
> stay legible from 10% to 1600% (adaptive subdivision)."

Built. Tick placement lives in `engine/canvas/ruler.ts` and is pure, so the
legibility claim is proved by arithmetic across all three units and ten zoom
levels rather than eyeballed: a labelled tick always has 56px, a subdivision
always has 6px, and the step ladder is monotonic so zooming in never jumps to
a coarser step.

Steps are chosen in the DISPLAY unit, not in points — a millimetre ruler must
land on whole millimetres, and inches subdivide in halves down to sixteenths
rather than the decimal 1-2-5 run.

The drawing area is inset by the rulers rather than overlaid by them, so
nothing can hide underneath where it cannot be clicked. This shifted the fit
zoom, which in turn broke two e2e specs that assumed fixed pixel offsets —
both now derive their clicks from the live zoom.

Unblocks INC-2: drag-from-ruler is the missing half of guide creation.

### INC-2 · Guides cannot be created · CLOSED
All three parts built now INC-1 unblocked the first.

- **Drag from a ruler** creates a guide on the matching axis.
- **Snap indicators** are drawn from the `matches` that `snap()` has always
  returned and nothing ever showed — the canvas snapped silently.
- **Ctrl suspends snapping** for the duration of a drag, which is how you
  place something a hair off a guide deliberately.

Guides are an SVG overlay, not Fabric objects: they must never reach the scene
graph and therefore the PDF, must not be snap targets for themselves, and
keeping them out preserves the one-to-one Fabric↔scene mapping the sync
depends on. They are not pushed onto the history stack either — scaffolding
the user puts up and takes down would bury the edits that matter.

The lines are SVG but the hit targets are real buttons: a 1px line is not
something anyone can click, and an SVG element with a click handler is
pointer-only, where §4.7 wants keyboard removal too.

### INC-3 · Pan by space-drag and middle-drag · CLOSED
> "space-drag and middle-drag pan"

Both built. Space-drag sets `skipTargetFind` as well as clearing `selection`,
so a drag that starts over an object pans rather than selecting it — without
that the gesture only worked on empty pasteboard, which is where it is least
needed. Space is ignored while typing, and the held state is released on
window blur so alt-tabbing mid-drag cannot leave the canvas stuck in pan mode.

### INC-4 · Layers panel is read-only · CLOSED
> "Reorder by drag, rename, toggle visibility/lock, select."

Rename (double-click or F2, Escape abandons), visibility and lock toggles, and
reorder. All four are undoable.

Reorder is by keyboard and toolbar rather than drag — Ctrl+[ and Ctrl+] with
Shift for front and back. A drag-reorder in a tree is pointer-only unless it is
built twice, and §4.7 asks for the keyboard path regardless.

Hiding or locking a group applies to its children too: a hidden group whose
contents still draw would be a lie the canvas tells.

### INC-5 · Data grid: column resize · CLOSED
> "Sort, column resize, inline cell edit, row add/delete."

Drag the grip on a header's trailing edge, or focus it and use the arrow keys.
Clamped between 56 and 600px: a column dragged to nothing can never be grabbed
again, and one dragged enormously wide pushes every other off screen.

The grip carries 24px of hit area for WCAG 2.2 target size while drawing as a
2px rule, so the table still looks like a table.

### INC-6 · Data grid: no "add row" control · CLOSED
Add and delete buttons in the grid footer. `addRow` always worked; the control
was lost when the studio shell replaced the single-panel page, and Shift+Delete
is not something anyone discovers unaided.

### INC-7 · CSV column mapping is not editable · CLOSED
> "column mapping UI (source → target, or 'create column', or 'ignore') →
> type confirmation → import with progress"
> "Mapping is fully keyboard-operable. Inferred types are shown and
> overridable."

Each preview column now carries a target selector (an existing field, a new
column, or ignore) and a type override, with the user's corrections overlaid on
the proposal rather than replacing it — a fresh file still gets a sensible
starting point. The confirm row shows the row count while the import runs.

Fixed while building it: choosing "New column" set `target: null`, and
`importCsv` filters out null-target mappings as unmapped, so the column would
have been silently dropped rather than created.

### INC-8 · Record source has no UI · CLOSED by P9.3
`runRecordSource` and `columnSchemaFor` were built and tested but called from
nowhere. The SQL console now drives them: `SqlConsole.tsx` runs any read-only
query and "Use as record source" hands it to `setRecordSource`.

### INC-9 · Align and distribute have no keyboard shortcuts · CLOSED
Ctrl+Shift+L/C/R and T/M/B align; Ctrl+Shift+H and V distribute. Ctrl+[ and
Ctrl+] reorder within the stack, with Shift for front and back. All listed in
the shortcut reference, since an undiscoverable shortcut is one nobody uses.

### INC-10 · Nudge is not coalesced in history · CLOSED
> "A drag is one entry, not one per mousemove."

Fabric emits `object:modified` once per drag, so **drags are fine**. Held
arrow keys are not: each keypress pushes its own history entry, so nudging an
object ten steps costs ten undos. `coalesceKey` is implemented and tested in
the history module but no call site passes one.

**Fixed.** `nudge` passes `nudge:<selection-ids>`, so a run of arrow presses is
one history entry and changing the selection starts a new one. Covered in
`engine/store/nudge.test.ts`, including that a nudge does not merge into the
placement before it.

### INC-11 · Assets and fonts are not collected into a save · CLOSED
Assets and uploaded fonts both travel inside the `.toke`. Bundled faces stay
out — a project referencing a font this build ships can always find it, where
one referencing a user's own file would otherwise open with the wrong typeface
everywhere.

Fonts are restored before the scene loads, since text measured against a
substituted face would be wrong until something re-rendered.


### INC-13 · Multi-design projects cannot be created or switched · CLOSED
A switcher in the header adds, renames, switches and removes designs. The open
design is the live canvas; the rest are parked in `useDesignStore` and written
back into their own slots on save, so saving can no longer lose a design just
because it was not on screen. The last design cannot be removed — a project
with none has nothing to print.

### INC-14 · No single-writer lock for autosave · CLOSED
`claimWriter` in `engine/persistence/autosave.ts` takes a Web Lock for the
tab's lifetime — the native single-writer primitive, which the browser also
releases when a tab crashes, so no heartbeat protocol over BroadcastChannel is
needed. A second tab reports `AUTOSAVE_OTHER_TAB` in diagnostics and skips its
writes; skipped edits stay pending, so when the first tab closes the second
takes over and flushes them. Tested in `writerLock.test.ts` against Node's
real `navigator.locks`.

### INC-15 · Only the bundled Plex faces load · CLOSED
TrueType and OpenType upload, with a family picker that reads the font
REGISTRY rather than a hardcoded list — so it cannot offer a face the PDF
renderer is unable to embed.

Fixed a real bug this exposed: `fontsForExport` sent `font.family`, the name
the file calls itself, rather than `cssFamily`, the name the design references.
The worker could not find the face, so **any** uploaded font failed at export
with `PDF_FONT_MISSING`.

### INC-16 · Bundled fonts are WOFF, not TTF/OTF · DONE · 2026-09-22
**Fixed.** Switched to @expo-google-fonts, which ships TTF. Verified the
metrics are byte-identical to the WOFF first (zero delta across all three
faces), so the P5.3 agreement still holds — and now the SAME file is measured,
served to the browser and embedded in the PDF. @fontsource removed.


### INC-18 · Record source is still the raw guests table · DONE · 2026-09-21
**Fixed in P7.1.** `useDataStore` now holds `recordSource`, `records` and
`recordColumns` separately from the grid's table view, validated read-only on
the main thread before the query is sent. Live Mode and pre-flight both read
`records`.

Still open as INC-21: there is no UI to EDIT the record source, so it is
always the default `SELECT * FROM guests`.

### INC-19 · Overflow is not shown in the binding panel · CLOSED
The panel now names the record and the size it had to shrink to, and shows
resolution errors alongside parse errors. It is the surface actually looking
at the object, so it was the one place the information was missing.

### INC-21 · No record-source editor · CLOSED by P9.3
The SQL console is the editor. A design can print only accepted guests by
running the filter and pressing "Use as record source"; the templates ship
with exactly that query. A simpler filter control on top of it remains
possible but is no longer blocking anything.

### INC-22 · Pre-flight does not check images or fonts · CLOSED
`structuralFindings` in `preflight.ts` reports an image whose asset is not in
the asset store (`asset`) and a text node whose face cannot be found at all
(`font`). Structural findings are per node, not per record: a missing logo is
one problem, not 500. The report lists both kinds with a count.

### INC-23 · Cover images overflow their frame in the PDF · CLOSED
The renderer now emits a clip path for cover-fit images, balanced with the
graphics state so it cannot leak into the next card on the sheet. The fit
maths moved to `engine/scene/image.ts`, which the canvas and the PDF renderer
both call — one definition of what 'cover' means.

### INC-24 · Export sends every registered font, not just the used faces · CLOSED
`fontsForExport(nodes)` walks the scene and ships only the faces `getFont`
actually resolves to — the substituted face, not the requested one, since
that is what the worker will draw with. Unit-tested in `exportClient.test.ts`.

### INC-26 · Export has no memory ceiling · CLOSED
`assemble.golden.test.ts` now samples peak heap during a 500-record export
and asserts it under 1GB, alongside the existing 30s assertion. It passes
with wide margin, so incremental save stays unbuilt until a real run says
otherwise.

### INC-27 · Proof export is not reachable from the UI · CLOSED
"Proof this record" in the export dialog runs the worker's `proof` op against
the record currently being previewed and downloads `toke-proof.pdf` — one page
at trim size, no imposition, no marks.

### INC-33 · Undo was a no-op for every inspector edit · CLOSED
The replacement command built its inverse lazily — `previous ??= document.nodes`
inside `invert()` — and `invert` is called at UNDO time, after `apply` has
already changed the state. It captured the new nodes and "restored" them, so
binding edits, align, distribute, reorder and group were undoable in name only.
Never caught, because the undo tests all used `addNode`/`deleteSelection`,
which compute their inverses from the node itself.

Fixed by deleting `invert` entirely: the history stack already snapshots the
state before every command, and a coalesced gesture's entry deliberately holds
the state from before the WHOLE gesture — which no per-command inverse can
reconstruct. Less code and correct for drags.

### INC-34 · Image crop is fit-mode only, no crop rectangle · CLOSED
`ImageNode` carries a `crop` rectangle, edited as percentages in the inspector.
Expressed as fractions of the natural size so a crop survives the picture being
swapped for a different resolution of the same shot.

`.toke` schemaVersion is 2, with a migration that fills in the full-image crop
when reading a v1 file — every consumer sees current-format nodes, so nothing
downstream checks the version.

There is still no drag-a-box-over-the-image gesture; the numbers are typed.

### INC-35 · Image resize does not lock aspect · CLOSED
Shift-drag on a corner handle locks the aspect ratio, for every object rather
than only images. Fabric's default was the other way round — corners preserved
aspect and Shift freed them — which is backwards from every design tool and
silently distorted a Stretch-fit photo.


### INC-30 · Font weight 500 is not bundled · CLOSED
Worse than recorded. `getFont` fell back to the nearest loaded face, so
**fontkit measured Plex 400 while the browser's own CSS matching fell back to
a system font** — auto-fit passed against one typeface and the canvas drew
another, at roughly double the width. Caught by looking at a screenshot, not
by any test.

Fixed by bundling `plex-sans-500-normal.ttf`, with a fitness test asserting
that every weight the scene graph can ask for is actually shipped and that
each bundled URL resolves to a file in `public/`.

### INC-31 · Pre-flight does not check font availability · CLOSED
Pre-flight compares the face each text node asks for with the face `getFont`
returns and reports a substitution ("IBM Plex Sans 500 is not loaded;
printing in 400 instead") before export, not after the cards are printed.

### INC-20 · Token errors do not reach a diagnostics surface · CLOSED by P9.2
Resolution errors and auto-fit overflow now report to the diagnostics panel
with the offending objectId, which makes each finding click-to-focus. The
object still falls back to its template on canvas — a vanished object is
harder to diagnose than a wrong one — but the reason no longer disappears
with it.

