## 4. Claimed but never verified

Acceptance criteria that were written into the plan and not actually
exercised. Each needs a test, not an opinion.

### VER-1 · Canvas mount/unmount leak · CLOSED
`e2e/remount.spec.ts` unmounts the whole studio 50 times (by crossing the
1024px breakpoint) and asserts window and document listener counts, read from
Chrome's DevTools protocol, and the canvas element count are unchanged. A
mutation check — one missing `removeEventListener` — fails it at 22 → 122.
Also found DEF-4.

### VER-2 · Data grid at 10,000 rows · CLOSED
> "10,000 rows scroll at 60fps."

Exercised with 10,000 rows, asserting the DOM node count stays bounded rather
than measuring frame timing — a windowed grid renders a screenful whatever the
dataset is, and timing assertions flake in CI for reasons unrelated to the
code.

### VER-3 · Multi-select resize origin · CLOSED
`e2e/selection.spec.ts` resizes a two-object selection from its corner and
asserts the top-left member stays put while every size and gap scales by the
same factor. It failed first: see DEF-5.

### VER-4 · Marquee selection · CLOSED
`e2e/selection.spec.ts`: a marquee around two objects selects both; one over
empty pasteboard selects nothing.

### VER-7 · Measurement agreement on a non-bundled font · CLOSED
Now reachable, because INC-15 made font upload exist. An uploaded TTF
round-trips through save and reopen with identical measurements, and is
embedded as a subset in the exported PDF rather than substituted.

### VER-6 · Autosave under a real storage quota · CLOSED
`e2e/quota.spec.ts` shrinks the origin's quota through Chrome's DevTools
protocol and asserts the diagnostics panel says storage is full. It failed
first: see DEF-6.

### VER-5 · Numeric fields during a live drag · CLOSED
> "Values update live during canvas drag without fighting user input
> mid-edit."

Both halves hold now. The canvas publishes a `liveTransform` during a move or
scale which the inspector prefers over the scene node, so the fields track the
gesture. It is deliberately NOT the scene graph: writing every mousemove into
`nodes` would push a command per frame and re-run the store→Fabric sync
against the object the user is holding.

Asserted mid-gesture with the mouse still down, plus that the committed value
matches what the drag showed and that a whole drag is still one undo.

